# Known Patterns - spoke-triage
# ==============================================================================
# Institutional knowledge folded into triage-analyst's cached system prompt
# (docs/spec.md §6.1, §9) — NOT parsed into `verdict` rows at migration time.
# Computing a `verdict.template_hash` requires an actually-observed
# (service_name, logger, normalized_template) tuple; this file is prose
# read by the model, not a suppression list. To suppress a specific
# template outright, use `triage-cli verdict set <hash> benign` instead —
# that goes in the database and is excluded from the API call entirely
# (cheaper than asking the model to recognize it every run).
#
# Copy this file to `known_patterns.md` (gitignored, site-specific) and
# customize for your instance. triage-analyst loads it automatically if
# present at TRIAGE_KNOWN_PATTERNS_PATH; a missing file just means an
# unaugmented (still fully functional) system prompt.
#
# Format: one `## heading` per pattern, prose underneath describing when
# it's benign vs. when it's genuinely concerning. Be specific about literal
# log text to expect — the model is instructed to quote evidence, not infer.
# ==============================================================================

## Example: Service WebSocket Reconnection (LOW/INFO)
Some services maintain persistent WebSocket connections that periodically
cycle. This produces warning-level "close" and "broken pipe" log entries.
These are normal if the service reconnects successfully afterward. Only
escalate if reconnection fails or downstream services are affected.

## Example: Database Client Disconnects During Restarts (contextual)
When a database is intentionally restarted, client containers will log
FATAL connection errors. These are expected during maintenance windows.
Only flag as CRITICAL if they persist beyond a few minutes.

## Example: Log Shipper Old Sample Rejection (LOW)
If the log shipper (Alloy/Promtail) was offline, it may try to ship stale
logs that exceed the retention window. The log store rejects them. This is
a one-time catch-up event, not ongoing data loss.

## Anti-Hallucination: Framework "signals" Are Not Unix Signals
Django (and some other frameworks) use the word "signals" for a pub/sub
decorator pattern. Log lines like "Imported related module X.signals" or
loggers named "*.signals" are framework code, NOT Unix process signals. Do
NOT report SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGKILL events based on these lines. A genuine
process-signal event will contain the uppercase signal name, "Received
signal N", "forwarding signal", an OOM keyword ("Out of memory: Killed
process", "oom-killer"), or a worker-lifecycle line ("Shutting down",
"Stopping worker", "Worker stopped").
